<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ögedei Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1186–<a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ögedei Khagan, Ogodei · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ögedei Khan was the second khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, whose expansion he continued. He made his military reputation in the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Gurganj</a> in 1221, and was elected khan in 1229 after a two-year regency under his brother Tolui. His reign saw the start of the Mongol invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1231, the conquest of the Jin dynasty in 1234 and campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. With officials such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-chucai">Yelü Chucai</a> he organised taxation and regional bureaucracies, and he founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> in the 1230s. He died in 1241 and was succeeded by his son Güyük.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan.jpg" alt="Ögedei Khan" width="330" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumOgedeiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mongols under <b>Ögedei Khan</b> conquered the Jin dynasty in 1233/34.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Two armies sent by <b>Ögedei Khan</b>, one led by himself and one by his brother Tolui, converged under Subutai and began the siege of Kaifeng, the capital of the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-8">8 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
